#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069609} :activity/id #uuid "8ab5b031-09a4-5e75-b365-2c7486f2168f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975 :topic/id #uuid "7697398d-61ca-52dc-8fab-4d569b573455" :topic/math-academy-id 459} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070033 :tutorial/content "To find the distance between two points on the Cartesian plane, we can create a right triangle and use the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the points $(2, 3)$ and $(5, 7)$, shown in the diagram below. We draw a right triangle so that the segment between the two points is the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/3d/3d3629fbd659ebfb6d32080feeb64bd2ebdd3fb63e9826970d64aa41a00b9a56.png)\n\nWe can find the distance between the points by finding the length of the hypotenuse $d$ using the Pythagorean theorem in the form\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\n$$\n\nFrom this perspective:\n\n- $a$ is the distance between the $x$-coordinates.\n$a|= x_{2} - x_{1}; = 5 - 2; = 3$\n- $b$ is the distance between the $y$-coordinates.\n$b|= y_{2} - y_{1}; = 7 - 3; = 4$\n\nSo the distance between the points $(2, 3)$ and $(5, 7)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} d &= \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{9 + 16} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{25} \\\\[3pt] &= 5. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the distance $d$ between any two points in the Cartesian plane is given by the **distance formula**:\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9d89e3c-10fa-540c-bc9f-221d84f3a839" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9120 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8fd761d6-3af8-51f6-824f-a972e67c1b97" :step/math-academy-id 147701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068508 :question/id #uuid "ca739248-c388-531e-ba5f-7f9cce2aa70b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14706"} :knowledge-poi